I've identified a bug in the `NoteKit.swift` file where tags attached to other text (like `list,#urgent.` or `(#ideas)`) aren't being extracted because the current approach splits on spaces

Fixed and verified. The bug: `extractTags` split on spaces and required each token to *start* with `#`, so a tag glued to punctuation — `list,#urgent.` (token starts with `l`) or `(#ideas)` (token starts with `(`) — was silently dropped.

The fix in `swift/Sources/NoteKit/NoteKit.swift:10` replaces the split-with-prefix check with a single regex scan `#(\w+)` that finds a `#`-then-word-chars *anywhere* in the text, stopping at the first non-word character. That handles punctuation on either side (`#urgent.` → `urgent`, `(#ideas)` → `ideas`), keeps tags in appearance order (regex matches are returned in order), and never regresses the old behavior: a standalone token like `#world` matches exactly as before. A `#` with no word chars after it (a trailing `C#`, a lone `#`) isn't a tag, so it returns nothing — same as before (old code would have produced an empty tag for a bare `#` token, a latent bug this also fixes).

Verification: SwiftPM is sandbox-blocked in this run root, so I compiled the actual source plus a harness with the real `swiftc` and ran it. All 9 checks passed: the two pre-existing tests, both reported cases, order preservation (`done #b, and #a next` → `[b, a]`), multi-line, and edge cases. I also added those cases to `swift/Tests/NoteKitTests/NoteKitTests.swift` so they're covered when the suite runs. The temporary harness was removed; git shows only the two intended files modified.

One note: the rust twin `extract_tags` in `rust/src/lib.rs` has the identical bug, but it's outside the scope you gave me (Swift `NoteKit`), so I left it untouched.


